Do we need LLM to train an autonomous agent?
No, you do not strictly need a Large Language Model (LLM) to train an autonomous agent. 
Autonomous agents existed long before LLMs and can be trained using several alternative artificial intelligence architectures depending on the task. 
Alternative Training Methods
Reinforcement Learning (RL): Agents learn by trial and error using rewards.
Deep Q-Networks (DQN): Used for mastering video games like Atari.
Actor-Critic Models: Standard for robotics and physical navigation.
Supervised Learning: Training agents using labeled human demonstrations.
Finite State Machines: Rule-based systems for predictable environments. 
When LLMs Are Required
Natural Language Interfaces: Interacting with humans via text or speech.
Complex Reasoning: Breaking down multi-step abstract goals into tasks.
Tool Use: Figuring out how to use APIs and software applications. 
Traditional Agents vs. LLM Agents
Traditional Agents: Excellent for narrow, high-speed tasks like chess or driving.
LLM Agents: Excellent for open-ended, creative, or language-heavy tasks. 
